# FPS_Controls.events — the reusable first-person controller (Task_FPS Phases 1–3).
#
# Nothing in here is specific to one scene: it is the input half of the controller, written
# once and pulled into a player sheet with a single `include "FPS_Controls"` line. The other
# half already lives in C# and is shared the same way — physics mode and ground probe
# (`PhysicsMovement`, `CharacterStep`), stance (`Stance`), pace/stamina (`Sprint`), look
# (`MouseLook`) and the camera rig (`CameraState`) — so a new first-person character needs a
# Camera child, the EventSheet tick boxes (First Person, Use Physics Movement, Move Relative
# To Camera) and a sheet that includes this file.
#
# What the including sheet is expected to provide:
#   · `my Pace`  — the speed every move asks for. If it is never set, every move runs at 0.
#   · optionally `my SprintSpeed` (default 2), `my MaxStamina` (default 100) and
#     `my Stamina` (set it to `my MaxStamina` on start to have a bar at all; with no
#     `my Stamina` a sprint is free).
#   · its own `when game starts` block with `lock cursor` if it wants the pointer captured —
#     capture belongs to the game's menu policy, not to the movement library.
#
# Keys: W/A/S/D move, Left Shift sprints, Left Ctrl ducks, Space jumps when grounded,
# Escape hands the pointer back and takes it again on the next press.

# Every move asks for `my Pace`; sprint is a multiplier on top (see Sprint.cs), so the speed
# lives in one place and Shift never needs its own copy of the W line. Move Relative To Camera
# is what makes A/D a strafe instead of a turn.
when W is held
  move forward speed my Pace

when S is held
  move backward speed my Pace

when A is held
  move left speed my Pace

when D is held
  move right speed my Pace

# Pace: `my Pace` × `my SprintSpeed`. The bridge spends `my Stamina` (50/s moving) and refills
# it walking (16/s); an empty bar drops back to walk, and `sprint` refuses to restart until
# some has come back.
when Left Shift is pressed
  sprint

when Left Shift is released
  walk

# Hold Ctrl to duck. The bridge shortens the capsule, sinks the body (so the camera child
# comes down with it) and halves whatever speed the moves ask for.
when Left Ctrl is pressed
  crouch

when Left Ctrl is released
  stand

# `jump` already refuses a mid-air take-off; the gate says so out loud, so a future
# `jump again` (double jump) stays a deliberate exception.
when Space is pressed
  me is on ground
  jump

# One key flips capture, so a menu or the debugger stays usable.
when Escape is pressed
  toggle cursor lock
